Constraints

Constraints are rules applied to columns.

They help maintain data integrity.

Why Constraints?

Without constraints, someone could insert:

student_id	name
NULL	Nanda
1	Ravi
1	Sai

Problems:

NULL IDs
Duplicate IDs

Constraints prevent this.

1. NOT NULL

Ensures a column cannot have NULL values.

Example
CREATE TABLE Students (
    student_id INT,
    name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL
);
Allowed
INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (1, 'Nanda');
Not Allowed
INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (2, NULL);

❌ Error because name cannot be NULL.

2. UNIQUE

Ensures all values in a column are different.

Example
CREATE TABLE Users (
    user_id INT,
    email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE
);
Allowed
nanda@gmail.com
ravi@gmail.com
Not Allowed
nanda@gmail.com
nanda@gmail.com

❌ Duplicate value.

Difference Between PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE
PRIMARY KEY	UNIQUE
Cannot be NULL	Can contain NULL (DB dependent)
Only one per table	Multiple allowed
Always unique	Also unique
3. DEFAULT

Assigns a value automatically if none is provided.

Example
CREATE TABLE Employee (
    emp_id INT,
    salary INT DEFAULT 30000
);
Insert
INSERT INTO Employee(emp_id)
VALUES (1);
Result
emp_id	salary
1	30000

Because SQL automatically used the default value.

4. CHECK

Ensures values satisfy a condition.

Example
CREATE TABLE Students (
    student_id INT,
    age INT CHECK(age >= 18)
);
Allowed
age = 20

✅

Not Allowed
age = 15

❌ Violates condition.

Visual Summary
NOT NULL
   ↓
Value required

UNIQUE
   ↓
No duplicates

DEFAULT
   ↓
Automatic value

CHECK
   ↓
Must satisfy condition
Real Interview Example
CREATE TABLE Users (
    user_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    username VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE,
    age INT CHECK(age >= 18),
    role VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'User'
);

This single table uses almost every important constraint.

